From f1d365ea3e1765a0d951cfef9d69f113d1f8f3d7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vortiago <atle@havso.net>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 13:43:50 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(ggml-cpu): share one work list across small experts in
 mul_mat_id
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

At decode and MTP verify an expert gets 1 to 4 routed rows. The per
expert plan then has fewer than 4 chunks per thread, so it slices the
rows of every expert once per thread: with 10 experts and 16 threads,
each thread reads 10 short slices (40 of 640 rows), one in each expert.

These small experts now share one work list of (expert, row chunk)
pairs, taken from the threadpool chunk counter in expert order. Each
small expert splits into the fewest chunks that make the total a
multiple of nth, so the threads get equal shares: for 10 experts and
16 threads that is 8 chunks of 80 rows, and a thread reads 5 experts
in runs twice as long. The counter also lets a thread that runs late
hand its share to the others. When the number of small experts and
nth have no common factor, the split is the old one per thread, and
only the work stealing is new.

Experts with enough rows keep their own chunk counter, tiled keeps its
per expert dispatch, and NUMA keeps the per thread slices. Tiled can
take an expert that the count includes: the list is then shorter and
may not divide evenly, which costs balance, not correctness. The per
expert plan moves into ggml_mmid_plan_expert, so the flat path and the
per expert path agree on which experts are small.

Each output is still one vec_dot over a full row, so the result is
bit-exact. The speed gain is not benchmarked yet.

The test cases add the up/gate (640 rows) and down (2560 rows) shapes
of Qwen3.8-Flash-Next at batch 1, 2, 4 and 8. In a CPU-only build the
test mode skips them, because the CPU backend is the reference. The
bit-exact check against the unpatched CPU backend used an out-of-tree
dump harness, at 1, 2, 3, 7, 12, 16, 32 and 64 threads.

On top of the llamafile_sgemm path for Q8_0 experts, the experts that
path computes stay out of the flat list and out of its count, so the
list divides over the threads as it would without them. The sgemm path
uses its own item counter in the work buffer, not the threadpool chunk
counter, and its one barrier is reached by every thread or by none.

